<h2>The marriage and the trial decree</h2>
<p>The parties married on 21 October 1993 at St. Xavier&rsquo;s Church, Punnakayal; the marriage produced no children. In 2017 the husband petitioned for dissolution (D.O.P. No. 82 of 2017, Principal District Court, Thoothukudi), alleging a long course of conduct: insults, persistent suspicion of his fidelity, refusal to cook, incitement of her family members who assaulted him, and desertion from January 2017. The trial court granted the decree on 24 February 2023. The wife appealed under Section 55 of the Divorce Act, 1869, denying the allegations and alleging in turn that the husband had sold her jewels in 2009.</p>

<h2>The finding that decided the appeal</h2>
<p>Among the instances of cruelty, one remark stood out: the wife had told the husband to go and sleep with his mother. The Court described the remark as unacceptable — in its words, it <em>&ldquo;cannot be digested&rdquo;</em> — and, critically, noted that the husband&rsquo;s testimony on it was not challenged in cross-examination. Under the ordinary rules of evidence, testimony on a material fact that the opposite party declines to confront stands proved. The single remark did not stand alone; it crowned a proved pattern of humiliation and hostility.</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Allegation pleaded</strong> — the husband&rsquo;s petition set out specific instances of insult and humiliation, with the offending remark identified.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Evidence led</strong> — he deposed to those instances as PW-1, exposing the account to cross-examination.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>No challenge</strong> — the remark went unconfronted in cross-examination, so the testimony stood unimpeached.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Finding</strong> — the conduct, assessed cumulatively, amounted to mental cruelty; the decree was upheld.</div>
</div>

<h2>The counter-allegation that failed</h2>
<p>The wife&rsquo;s principal answer was that the husband had sold her jewels in 2009. The Court rejected it for want of the basic particulars that make such a claim provable: the nature of the jewels, their value, and what became of the sale proceeds. A matrimonial counter-allegation is not a makeweight — it must be proved like any other fact, and an unproved accusation can itself colour the court&rsquo;s assessment of the marriage.</p>
<p>Her conduct after separation compounded the difficulty. She had left in January 2017, lodged no complaint about the jewels, and never filed a petition for restitution of conjugal rights. Relying on Supreme Court authority cited in the judgment — <strong>Gurbux Singh v. Harminder Kaur</strong>, (2010) 14 SCC 301 — the Court observed that cruelty differs from person to person and situation to situation: <em>&ldquo;cruelty is different from different persons and the conduct of the person in different situation.&rdquo;</em> On the whole record, the appeal failed and the decree of divorce stood.</p>

<h2>Lessons for matrimonial trials</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Cross-examine the sting.</strong> Every specific utterance or incident attributed to a party must be squarely challenged in cross-examination. Silence converts an allegation into a finding.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Prove counter-allegations properly.</strong> A claim that stridhan or jewels were misappropriated needs particulars — description, value, dates and follow-up action — or it will be discarded.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Conduct is evidence.</strong> Years of separation without a complaint or a restitution petition can be read against a spouse who claims willingness to cohabit.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Cruelty is contextual.</strong> Courts weigh the parties&rsquo; background and the cumulative effect of conduct, not isolated incidents against a fixed yardstick.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">This article summarises a judgment of the Madras High Court (Madurai Bench) for general information. Personal-law statutes differ — the Divorce Act, 1869 governs Christian marriages, while the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955 and other personal laws carry their own cruelty jurisprudence — so the governing statute should always be identified first.</div>
